WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.615
Between visits, patients make health decisions on their own. Their clinic rarely hears about them.

00:00:06.615 --> 00:00:16.680
Only eighteen percent of supplement users had told a provider. And supplements send about twenty-three thousand Americans to the emergency room each year.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:22.280
Ladder closes that loop, built on JupyterHealth.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.642
Meet Alex. He’s 37, has type 2 diabetes, and takes metformin.

00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:33.422
A video tells him berberine is “nature’s Ozempic.”

00:00:33.422 --> 00:00:38.422
Before acting on it, he checks the claim with Ladder.

00:00:38.422 --> 00:00:44.622
Ladder shows what the studies actually found, and where the video stretched them.

00:00:44.622 --> 00:00:49.022
And it never tells him to stop his medicine.

00:00:49.222 --> 00:00:54.822
Alex is thinking about starting berberine and stopping metformin.

00:00:55.022 --> 00:00:58.422
Normally, his clinic would never hear about this.

00:00:58.422 --> 00:01:02.022
So Ladder asks: do you want your care team to know?

00:01:02.222 --> 00:01:07.249
He joins his clinic’s study on the JupyterHealth Exchange, choosing what to share.

00:01:07.249 --> 00:01:13.883
His medicines and lab results come from his patient portal, with his permission.

00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:16.883
Sent, with his consent.

00:01:17.083 --> 00:01:23.953
Behind the scenes, his decision is stored on the Exchange in FHIR format, for Dr. Lee’s care team.

00:01:23.953 --> 00:01:30.311
Our open question for Edge Medicine: store this as a MedicationStatement, or an Observation?

00:01:31.511 --> 00:01:36.111
The next day, Dr. Lee opens Alex’s chart.

00:01:36.111 --> 00:01:40.511
No supplements on record. But Ladder has a new signal.

00:01:41.511 --> 00:01:46.419
One card: his words, the evidence, and his medicines and A1c.

00:01:46.419 --> 00:01:51.019
No AI advice. The clinician decides.

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:55.619
Dr. Lee replies.

00:01:55.819 --> 00:02:01.019
Alex hears back three days after his decision, not at his next visit.

00:02:01.019 --> 00:02:06.392
The loop is closed.

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:13.228
What about a claim nobody has reviewed yet?

00:02:13.228 --> 00:02:18.228
Ladder gives no verdict until a licensed expert reviews it.

00:02:18.428 --> 00:02:24.428
And if Alex says no, nothing reaches the clinic.

00:02:24.428 --> 00:02:34.931
It runs on three pieces of JupyterHealth: the Exchange, a Voilà dashboard on JupyterHub, and SMART on FHIR. Nothing custom for each hospital.

00:02:34.931 --> 00:02:46.304
Phase one tests one question: will patients tell their care team? We'll measure the share rate and the time to the clinician, in a pilot designed with UCSF mentors.

00:02:46.304 --> 00:02:55.504
Ladder. Decided at home, seen in clinic.
